[section, $md:
    # Core packages

    Core packages provide typed capabilities owned by the compiler. Binding-backed
    Core packages are selected by the active builder. They use the same dependency
    syntax as source packages, but binding-backed implementations don't become
    files in your project.

    `@html` and `@styles` are different. They are mandatory Core/MothSource
    packages that the common builder surface registers under every builder,
    including custom SDK builders. Builder configuration never selects them, and
    `.mtf` templates always see their compile-time constants in the fixed
    prelude.

    Most other Core packages require explicit dependency clauses. Unlike large
    standard-library preludes, Moth reserves only a small implicit namespace
    surface such as `io`.

    Official environments target the complete Core catalogue with documented
    host-capability gates. Custom builders may supply only the implementations
    they need. The reserved `io` namespace alone does not guarantee that a custom
    host implements its operations.

    The @../../progress/packages-and-builders (packages and builders progress
    matrix) records current availability and implementation gaps. @../../lappet
    (Lappet) describes planned native Core support and official package admission.

    ## Package pages

    - @./html/ (@html)
    - @./styles/ (@styles)
    - @./prelude (Prelude policy)
    - @./io (Core IO)
    - @./collections (Core collections)
    - @./math (Core math)
    - @./text (Core text)
    - @./random (Core random)
    - @./time (Core time)
]

